architecture-decision-record:
  identifier: "0007"
  title: Remove the literal escape hatch in favour of YAML block scalars
  status: accepted
  date: "2026-08-21"
  deciders:
    - felix
  superseded-by: ""
  context: |
    SYON had two ways to write verbatim multi-line text. The first was Block
    3, the literal escape hatch: content delimited by [[[ and ]]] on their own
    lines. The second was the YAML block scalar, a | header whose body is the
    following more-indented lines. Both produced the same AST node,
    Value::LiteralBlock(String).

    Only one of them is valid YAML. ADR 0005 established that a document using
    a fence or a literal-block delimiter cannot be parsed by a YAML 1.2 parser
    at all, so those constructs cost the Block 1 compatibility guarantee. A |
    block scalar costs nothing: it is ordinary YAML.

    Because both spellings collapsed into one AST node, phase1 counted them in
    one bucket, and the yaml_compatible() predicate could not tell them apart.
    Commit 1d560c2 dropped that whole bucket from the predicate, which made |
    report correctly and made [[[ report incorrectly -- a document using the
    escape hatch was announced as YAML-compatible, contradicting ADR 0005. The
    phase1::tests::literal_block_reduces_compatibility test had been failing
    ever since, and was the only thing pointing at the gap. The Go
    implementation never received the change at all, so syon-go/phase1.go and
    crates/syon-parser/src/phase1.rs had been disagreeing since that commit --
    exactly the cross-implementation drift ADR 0004 exists to catch.

    Keeping both spellings meant either splitting the AST node to tell them
    apart, or leaving the predicate wrong. Neither is worth it for a construct
    whose only distinguishing feature is that it is not YAML.
  decision: |
    Remove [[[ ... ]]] from the language. The | block scalar, already
    implemented and already the better-behaved of the two, is the single way
    to write verbatim multi-line text. > remains accepted as a spelling of |;
    SYON still has no folded style and never folds newlines into spaces.

    Removing it rather than deprecating it keeps one spelling in the corpus
    and one code path in each implementation. The construct was
    SYON-specific, so nothing outside this project can be written in it.

    [[[ and ]]] are rejected by name, in both implementations, with an error
    that names the replacement. Falling through to the generic
    flow-sequence-not-allowed error would technically reject the input while
    telling the writer nothing about what to write instead.

    With Block 3 gone, phase1's block numbering no longer diverges from
    spec/02-grammar.md, and the report keys were renamed from block2/block3 to
    block-scalars/fences -- self-describing names, so the two numbering
    schemes ADR 0006 documented have nothing left to disagree about.
  consequences:
    positive:
      - The whole example corpus is now genuinely YAML-1.2-compatible. Both phase1 implementations independently report 8 of 8 files compatible at 100 percent, with identical block counts.
      - yaml_compatible() is true again to its own documentation. A fence is now the only construct that costs compatibility, which is a claim that can be stated in one sentence and checked in one line of code.
      - The Rust and Go implementations agree again on the compatibility model, and the Go parser gained | block scalar support, which it did not have at all before this change.
      - One less SYON-specific construct for a reader coming from YAML to learn, and one less code path in the lexer, the parser, the encoder and the analyzer of each implementation.
      - The deferred work item in README.md for a YAML-compatible mode for Block 3 (issue #3) is resolved, and more cheaply than a mode would have been - there is no mode to select.
    negative:
      - The 6 decision records and 1 glossary entry that used [[[ were rewritten to |. Their parsed values are equivalent apart from one intended difference - the escape hatch preserved each body line's absolute source indentation, whereas a block scalar dedents by the body's common leading indent. Verified equivalent under that normalisation before and after.
      - AGPL.license.syon was converted too, all 7 blocks, with each body shifted two columns deeper so it sits past the key that owns it. Its license text is byte-identical once indentation and the dropped closers are normalised. The file still does not parse, exactly as it did not before this change: a "date;" typo on line 5, a bare multi-line scalar under summary, and prose continuation lines written at the same indent as their own "- " marker where SYON requires them deeper. None of those involve literal blocks and none are fixed here.
      - phase1.report.syon changes shape - block2 and block3 became block-scalars and fences. Any consumer reading those keys must be updated. No such consumer is known outside this repository.
      - The compatibility percentage now counts block scalars in the numerator, so documents using them report higher than they did. This is the correction, not a regression, but archived reports are not comparable with new ones.
  alternatives-rejected:
    -
      name: Split Value::LiteralBlock so phase1 can tell [[[ from | and keep both
      reason: Restores the invariant but keeps two spellings of one idea, and adds a discriminant threaded through the AST, both implementations, the Python binding and the encoder -- all to preserve a construct whose only distinguishing property is that it is not YAML.
    -
      name: Deprecate [[[ with a warning and remove it in a later release
      reason: The construct is SYON-specific, so the entire population of documents using it is inside this repository and was converted in the same change. A deprecation period protects external users who cannot exist.
    -
      name: Leave the predicate as it is and delete the failing test
      reason: Would have left [[[ reporting as YAML-compatible in direct contradiction of ADR 0005, and deleted the only signal pointing at it.
